## The reader decision
<!-- claim -->
**{{ v('ch8.message') }}**
<!-- step: curve -->
Everything above is about places. This chapter is about a household. At the {{ v('policy.affordable_line') }} line, {{ v('policy.down_payment_share') }} down and the latest quarterly rate of {{ v('ch8.rate') }}, the median household can afford the median home in {{ v('ch8.median_can_afford.metros') }} of {{ v('ch8.metros') }} metros ({{ v('ch8.median_can_afford.share') }}); across metros the affordable price sits a median of {{ v('ch8.affordable_gap.median') }} from the median home, and a median of {{ v('ch8.affordable_share.median') }} of local households clear the payment on it. A renter at the renter median income saving {{ v('policy.saving_rate') }} of income needs {{ v('ch8.down_payment_years.median') }} to reach the down payment in the typical metro and {{ v('ch8.down_payment_years.max') }} in {{ v('ch8.down_payment_years.max_metro') }}. The chart draws the rent or buy question for {{ v('ch8.curve.metro') }}: the cumulative cost of owning net of the equity built, less the cumulative cost of renting with the down payment invested instead, year by year to a {{ v('ch8.horizon') }} year horizon. Where the curve crosses zero is the breakeven; here it is {{ v('ch8.curve.breakeven') }}.
<!-- step: breakeven -->
The paths behind the curve are not a forecast. Appreciation and rent growth are drawn in blocks of three years from the metro's own history, {{ v('ch8.draws') }} paths per metro, and the band is the spread of those paths. Across the metros with enough history, {{ v('ch8.metros_with_breakeven') }} of them, the owner weighted median breakeven is {{ v('ch8.breakeven.median') }} (interval {{ v('ch8.breakeven.low') }} to {{ v('ch8.breakeven.high') }}), and in {{ v('ch8.breakeven.never_share') }} of paths owning does not catch up inside the horizon at all. One point of rate moves the median breakeven by {{ v('ch8.breakeven.rate_move') }} years. The quickest breakevens among the largest metros are {{ v('ch8.quickest.1.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.quickest.1.years') }}), {{ v('ch8.quickest.2.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.quickest.2.years') }}) and {{ v('ch8.quickest.3.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.quickest.3.years') }}); the slowest are {{ v('ch8.longest.1.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.longest.1.years') }}), {{ v('ch8.longest.2.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.longest.2.years') }}) and {{ v('ch8.longest.3.metro') }} ({{ v('ch8.longest.3.years') }}). The planner page runs the same arithmetic for any metro, income, rate, down payment and horizon, through the live API, and logs the check so that the next quarter of prices and rates can grade it.
